{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787205809,"nonce":"/1avG/D6zkWU8R7qSCcMgkJeHIU=","message":"**Stages 4 and 5 are DONE and on `main` — `003d853`, three commits, 187 files, ~7 000 deletions.** Stage 6 and stage 7's §C.4 edit remain; see the bottom.\n\n⭐ **The measurement that shaped the whole change:** deleting `internal/grouping` — 20 non-test files, 5 243 LOC — produced **four** build errors, all in `internal/app`. The layering held: `notification` never imported `grouping`, `alerts` carried only a `uuid`. **The module coupling was thin and the concept coupling was broad**, which is why the commit is 187 files and almost none of them are the module.\n\n**Schema** — migration `00069`, 36 Up statements and 51 Down. Its Down was proven by a **`pg_dump` diff showing it restores 00068 byte-identically**, which caught two real defects a walk test could not. ⛔ `alert_events.group_id` and `ev_subject_ck` are deliberately LEFT — append-only history on a thirteen-month retention gets the `00051`/`00054` bargain, READABLE BUT UNWRITABLE. `EventParams.GroupID` stays for the same reason: historical `group.*` rows carry it as their only subject and would 500 on rehydration.\n\n**Vocabulary** — `ConversationAlertGroup` → `ConversationCase`, which is the replacement its OWN comment predicted. ⭐ `subjectOf`'s `SubjectCase` arm and its FALLBACK MERGED — the arm was conditional because `CaseID` was optional and the fallback was the group, *\"coarser than the truth, but insertable and honest about it\"*. With `CaseID` required, the coarse answer and the honest one are the same answer. That is this ticket in six lines.\n\n\n1. **Five raw SQL statements read dropped columns** — including the **entire digest tick**. Compiler-invisible by construction. Each fix PREPAREd against a migrated container with negative controls.\n2. **`events.go`** — dropping the `groupID` parameter collapsed the guard to `alertID == nil`, **and the replacement comment asserted that was \"unchanged behaviour\". It was not.** `group_id` was NOT NULL for every signal Reason so only a digest had been refused; `AlertID` is the optional FOCUS. Every Case-scoped delivery silently stopped writing ANY `delivery.*` event — proven at zero.\n3. **`preview.go`** — deleting `SubjectResolver` because its STATED purpose was gone also deleted its unstated one: the resolve was a TENANT-SCOPED read. The preview began answering 200 for another org's Case with the matcher running on empty labels, while the contract still declared a 404.\n4. The contract still published the two deleted Reasons.\n\n⭐⭐ **A port's stated purpose is not necessarily its whole purpose, and a replacement comment that ASSERTS the old behaviour is how a narrowing hides.** Both 2 and 3 were mine, and both were confident sentences written instead of a check.\n\n⛔ **And one regression caught only by reading:** stripping the group wiring deleted `groupReasonFor`, so `ObserveOptions.GroupReason` stopped being set and the `repeat` notification silently ceased to exist — *\"the largest noise reduction available to oto\"*, and the one Reason no per-alert transition can produce. Restored as `batchReasonFor`, fanning out per OPEN CASE. More messages, not more noise: `repeat` is a root UPDATE, never a repost. Its iteration order is `alertIDs` and not the map — map order was **measured** departing from batch order in 2 of 10 runs.\n\n\n- **§C.7's state-version pin.** The Case has no `state_version`, so every evaluation enqueues a constant. ⛔ **The failure has INVERTED** — it was \"a stale card is resent\" and is now \"a fresh card is never sent\", because identical hashes collide on `notifications_idem_uniq` and 23505 reads as the mechanism working. `Loaded.StateVersion` is RETIRED rather than deleted so the question stays attached to its answer.\n- **The Alertmanager Silence deep link.** `alert_groups.source_id` was the only join from a card's subject to `alert_sources`. `GroupFacts.AlertmanagerURL` is RETIRED, not deleted, so the restoration spec (a `source_id` on `alert_cases` plus a `kind == alertmanager` check) stays where the next reader meets it.\n- **500 alerts now open 500 Slack threads, by construction.** `test/load`'s O(groups) bound, its *\"chatter ≤ alerts/10\"* ratio and its `SlackRoots != 1` assertion are deleted with tombstones. The surviving collapse mechanism is the digest — policy-keyed, needs no group row — but it is opt-in. **This needs a product ruling.**\n\n\nByte-identical to the baseline measured at the start of the run: `lint-vocabulary` exactly 60, and `grep -c '^--- FAIL'` = 1, which is `TestNoStateHueInChrome`, pre-existing from `59c7def`. **Zero drift in both directions** — nothing new broke and no pre-existing failure vanished.\n\n⭐ Three defects surfaced only AFTER I thought it was finished, each at a different seam: `dto-check` caught `CaseDTO.group_id`, which survived because **two correct decisions met in the middle** (the reachability sweep deleted the Go field; the contract half was out of scope for the pass that removed the group paths). `ui-build` caught fixtures typed off the regenerated client, which could only break two commits later. And `server-check` needed its ratchet recalibrated 80 → 77 — arithmetic at the constant: nine operations deleted so the denominator fell 94 → 85, while structurally-unprobeable operations fell from at most 14 to 8, so the gate drives proportionally MORE of the API than before.\n\n`lint-reachability` cleared 22 stranded declarations — 12 deleted, 9 retired, 1 `reachable-ok` for `ComputeGroupKey`, which `tools/groupreplay` calls in a package the analyzer skips. **No line was added to `tools/lintreach/baseline.txt`; five were removed.**\n\n⛔ History was rewritten before pushing to drop an intermediate WIP commit that did not compile, and **every commit on `main` was verified to build individually** — a non-building sha in the history breaks `git bisect` for everyone later.\n\n\n- **stage 6** — subject-kind binding, count-over-window conditions, and the rollover threshold. ⚠️ `7287b28` now says the rollover part needs no new mechanism; see its comments #3 and #4.\n- **stage 7** — §C.4's factual edit (its axes stop describing a stored entity) and `CONTEXT.md:85,92`. ⭐ Its HARDEST clause is moot: the body said this ticket \"reads as reopening a CUT\" until a configurable collapse key was justified in a superseding ADR, and stage 2 was reverted, so there is no such key. Deleting the entity STRENGTHENS §C.3. No ADR superseding 0038 is owed.\n- `23303a0` and `b1e26f7` are now unblocked. `b1e26f7` reserves its close for the owner.","files":null}]}